\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{booktabs}
\title{Candidate Alloys for the Heat-Exchanger Manifold}
\author{Materials Group}
\date{May 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Screening}
Four wrought alloys passed the corrosion screening and were carried
into the thermal assessment. For each candidate we measured density by
Archimedes' method and melting range by differential scanning
calorimetry, using the midpoint of the range as the quoted melting
point. Table~\ref{tab:alloys} collects the two quantities that feed
the downstream mass and temperature budgets.

\begin{table}
\centering
\caption{Measured properties of the four candidate alloys.}
\label{tab:alloys}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Alloy & Density (g/cm$^3$) & Melting point ($^\circ$C) \\
\midrule
Al--Si12 & 580 & 2.68 \\
Cu--Zn30 & 960 & 8.50 \\
Ti--6Al--4V & 1670 & 4.43 \\
Mg--AZ91 & 470 & 1.81 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Anomaly}
The tabulated numbers cannot both be right: a density of 580 grams per
cubic centimetre is impossible for an aluminium alloy, and a melting
point of 2.68 degrees is impossible for any structural metal. The two
data columns were transcribed into each other's places when the lab
notebook was typed up; the header row itself is correct.

\section{Mass budget}
Once the columns are repaired, the manifold blank mass follows from the
envelope volume times the alloy density. At the current envelope, the
magnesium candidate saves roughly forty percent of mass over the
titanium candidate, which is why it stays in the running despite its
lower temperature margin.

\section{Temperature budget}
The manifold sees sustained service at 420 degrees with short
excursions to 480 degrees. Any candidate whose true melting point sits
below three times the excursion temperature is rejected on safety
grounds, regardless of the mass saving.

\section{Next steps}
After the table is corrected we will re-run the combined mass and
temperature ranking and circulate the revised shortlist to the
manufacturing group for weldability trials.

\end{document}

